parts.stem:
Amino acids can be separated by thin-layer chromatography. A mixture of amino acids is analysed using this technique.

The chromatogram obtained is shown, drawn to scale. The table shows some $R_{\text{f}}$ values for different amino acids in the solvent used.

{{figure:pdf_visual_01.png}}

parts.text:
Apart from glycine, all naturally occurring amino acids have a chiral centre and exhibit stereoisomerism.

Draw the two stereoisomers of alanine, $\text{CH}_3\text{CH(NH}_2\text{)CO}_2\text{H}$.

{{figure:pdf_visual_02.png}}

Serine, $\text{HOCH}_2\text{CH(NH}_2\text{)CO}_2\text{H}$, can react with alanine, $\text{CH}_3\text{CH(NH}_2\text{)CO}_2\text{H}$, to form three different **structural** isomers, each with the molecular formula $\text{C}_6\text{H}_{12}\text{N}_2\text{O}_4$.

Draw the structures of these three structural isomers.

{{figure:pdf_visual_03.png}}
